In Rust 2024, temporaries from the two halves of a let chain seem to have different lifetimes:

struct D(&'static str);
impl Drop for D { fn drop(&mut self) { println!("drop {}", self.0); } }
impl D { fn opt(&self) -> Option<i32> { Some(1) } }
fn mk(n: &'static str) -> D { D(n) }

if let Some(_x) = mk("B").opt() && mk("C").opt().is_some() {
println!("body");
}

prints:

drop C
body
drop B

C (the boolean operand) is dropped before the body, B (the let operand) survives through it. With locks this is a footgun:

if let Some(x) = *M.lock().unwrap() && *N.lock().unwrap() > 0 {
// M is still locked here, N is not — M.lock() again deadlocks
}

Is this asymmetry specified somewhere, or an artifact of how if let scrutinee temporaries are extended? Is the boolean operand's early drop guaranteed, or should I not rely on it?

I am maintaining an internal Go library, which has a pubsub package. Currently, it doesn't support message ordering, but I would like to enable it. I want to make it as simple as possible by using defaults where it makes sense - convention over configuration type thing.

I have a simple Publish function which accepts a topic and a message payload:

func (c *Client) Publish(ctx context.Context, topic string, message any) error {
data, err := json.Marshal(message)
if err != nil {
return fmt.Errorf("failed to marshal message: %w", err)
}

pubSubMsg := &pubsub.Message{
Data: data,
PublishTime: time.Now().UTC(),
Attributes: map[string]string{},
}

// publishers are created lazily
if _, ok := c.publishers.Load(topic); !ok {
c.publishers.Store(topic, c.client.Publisher(topic))
}

publisher, _ := c.publishers.Load(topic)
result := publisher.(*pubsub.Publisher).Publish(ctx, pubSubMsg)
}

I want to add some default ordering, so that a developer would only need to enable ordering on the subscription when creating it in GCP, and it would work out of the box. I was thinking of doing something like:

pubSubMsg := &pubsub.Message{
Data: data,
PublishTime: time.Now().UTC(),
Attributes: map[string]string{},
OrderingKey: defaultMessageOrderingKey, // setting a default key here
}

if _, ok := c.publishers.Load(topic); !ok {
p := c.client.Publisher(topic)
p.EnableMessageOrdering = true
c.publishers.Store(topic, p)
}

Testing this locally works fine, but it's hard to tell what the performance implications are. The docs haven't touched on such scenarios, and they mostly assume ordering is enable on both subscriber and publisher.

I would additionally expose a functional option which would let clients set a custom ordering key per message like so:

type publishConfig struct {
orderingKey string
}

type PublishOption func(*publishConfig)

func WithOrderingKey(orderingKey string) PublishOption {
return func(cfg *publishConfig) {
cfg.orderingKey = orderingKey
}
}

func (c *Client) Publish(ctx context.Context, topic string, message any, opts ...PublishOption) error {
cfg := &publishConfig{
orderingKey: defaultMessageOrderingKey,
}
for _, opt := range opts {
opt(cfg)
}

data, err := json.Marshal(message)
if err != nil {
return fmt.Errorf("failed to marshal message: %w", err)
}

pubSubMsg := &pubsub.Message{
Data: data,
PublishTime: time.Now().UTC(),
Attributes: map[string]string{},
OrderingKey: cfg.orderingKey,
}
}

Since currently none of our subscriptions have ordering enabled, this should just work out of the box, but also enable developers to easily enable message ordering in the future by simply configuring it when creating subscriptions on GCP and nothing else. Obviously, if they want more granular control, they can use the WithOrderingKey option.

Would this come with any significant performance tradeoffs?

